# Cloudflare Pages response headers. Vite copies `public/` to the root of
# `dist/`, which is where Pages looks for this file.
#
# Cache-Control is set per-path and NEVER on `/*`: Pages applies every matching
# rule, so a blanket value here would collide with the specific ones below.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

# Vite hashes these filenames, so a changed file is a changed URL and the old
# one can be kept forever. This is the whole reason the hash is in the name.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The meshes are NOT hashed — `catalog.json` names them literally, and the
# scanner, the docs and the repo all use the same paths (see the copyModels
# note in vite.config.ts). So they get a week rather than a year: long enough
# that placing parts costs nothing on a second visit, short enough that a
# re-scanned model reaches people without a cache-buster.
/models/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# The entry point must revalidate every time, or a deploy never reaches anyone
# already carrying it: index.html is what names the hashed bundle.
/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The two pictures OF the app rather than in it. Not hashed — `index.html` and
# every scraper name them literally — so they take the meshes' week rather than
# the bundle's year. Measured on the first deploy: without a rule of their own
# they fell through to Pages' platform default of max-age=0, which re-fetches a
# 64 kB card on every visit to serve a file that changes when the logo does.
/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/social-card.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
